{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Getting started · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Getting started with php-via</h1>
    <p>
        php-via is a PHP framework for server-side reactive UIs. Your app runs as a long-lived
        <a href="https://openswoole.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSwoole</a> server, and
        <a href="https://data-star.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Datastar</a> applies the server's
        updates in the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
        You write signals, actions and views in PHP. Each tab receives new HTML and signal values over one
        SSE stream, so you write no JavaScript, run no build step and keep no separate API.
    </p>

    <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>PHP 8.4+</li>
        <li><a href="https://openswoole.com/docs/get-started/installation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSwoole PHP extension</a> 26+</li>
        <li>Composer</li>
        <li><a href="https://twig.symfony.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Twig</a> <em>(optional: views that are PHP closures need nothing more, template views need <code>composer require twig/twig</code>; see <a href="/docs/twig">Twig templates</a>)</em></li>
        <li><a href="https://pecl.php.net/package/brotli" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brotli PHP extension</a> <em>(optional: static files get Brotli level 11 once it is loaded, pages and SSE with <code>Config::withBrotli()</code>, and a <code>.br</code> file next to an asset goes out as it is, also without the extension; see <a href="/docs/deployment#static-compression">Static compression</a>)</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="installation">Installation</h2>
    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
composer require mbolli/php-via
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        php-via serves Datastar 1.0.4 itself, so the browser side needs no install.
    </p>

    <h2 id="your-first-app">Your first app</h2>
    <p>
        Create <code>app.php</code> in a new directory and put this counter in it. It is a complete
        php-via app:
    </p>

    <div class="docs-demo">
        <div class="docs-demo-header">The counter, running on this server</div>
        <div class="docs-demo-body">
            {{ demo | raw }}
        </div>
    </div>

    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
php app.php
# [INFO] Via server started on http://0.0.0.0:3000
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>Open <a href="http://localhost:3000" target="_blank">localhost:3000</a> in your browser and click the button. A second tab starts again at 0, because each tab keeps its own count. That is <strong>TAB scope</strong>, the default.</p>
    <p>
        The action changes <code>count</code> and returns. php-via then sends the new value to the tab, and
        <code>data-text</code> shows it. <code>$count-&gt;ref()</code> writes the signal's reference for such
        expressions, and <code>$count-&gt;int()</code> writes the value into the first render. The view's HTML
        stays the same, so nothing renders again. When an action changes what the view renders, it calls
        <code>$c-&gt;sync()</code>, and Datastar puts the new HTML in place by the id of the view's top-level
        element, here <code>counter</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
    <p>A php-via page goes through these steps:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>php-via runs your page handler, puts the view into its shell and sends the page. The shell's
            <code>via_head</code> writes the tags that connect the tab, the signal values come along as JSON, and
            <code>via_foot</code> loads Datastar.</li>
        <li>Datastar opens an SSE stream to <code>/_sse</code>, and php-via renders the view again for it.</li>
        <li>A click makes Datastar POST to <code>/_action/increment</code>, with the tab's signals in the body.</li>
        <li>Your action runs. When it returns, the signals it changed go to the tab over the stream.</li>
        <li>Datastar updates the signals in the page. A <code>sync()</code> also sends the view's new HTML, and
            Datastar morphs only the elements that differ.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The page does not reload.</p>

    <h2 id="configuration">Configuring the server</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Config</code> holds the server's settings. Make every <code>with*()</code> call before
        <code>new Via($config)</code>: the constructor freezes the Config, and a <code>with*()</code> call after
        it throws a <code>LogicException</code>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withPort(8080)
    ->withDevMode(true); // for local work only

$app = new Via($config);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Dev mode logs more warnings, shows the exception of a page that fails instead of a bare
        <code>Internal Server Error</code>, and shows the default shell's Live Signals panel. Leave it off in
        production. The <a href="/docs/api#config">Config reference</a> lists the settings.
    </p>

    <h2 id="testing">Testing the counter</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Testing\TestApp</code> runs your pages in a test, without a server, through php-via's own page,
        action and SSE handlers. To share the page between the app and its tests, register it in a file of its
        own:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
<?php
// routes.php
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Context;
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Via;

return function (Via $app): void {
    $app->page('/', function (Context $c): void {
        // the counter from app.php
    });
};
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>app.php</code> then calls <code>(require __DIR__ . '/routes.php')($app);</code> before
        <code>$app-&gt;start()</code>, and a <a href="https://pestphp.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pest</a>
        test opens the page, clicks twice and reads the signal the browser holds:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
<?php
// tests/CounterTest.php
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Config;
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Testing\TestApp;

it('counts clicks', function (): void {
    $app = new TestApp(new Config(), require __DIR__ . '/../routes.php');

    $tab = $app->open('/');
    $tab->action('increment')->action('increment');

    expect($tab->signal('count'))->toBe(2);
    $app->shutdown();
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A <code>TestTab</code> also gives you the patches its stream received (<code>patches()</code>), the page
        as the server would render it now (<code>html()</code>), and <code>disconnect(expire: true)</code> to
        test a <a href="/docs/lifecycle#revival">revival</a>. TestApp runs everything in one process as one
        worker.
    </p>

